    ('cc89fe8f-328b-470e-adcd-0d1244f28acf'::uuid, 'period_name', '1500+ AD', NULL, 'fields-wd3-2026-10-02b-s010-rollback:cc89fe8f-328b-470e-adcd-0d1244f28acf:period_name', 'rollback of fields-wd3-2026-10-02b-s010: period_name None restored on Richmond Park', '[{"source": "pipeline/utils/text.py:categorize_period", "url": "pipeline/utils/text.py", "quote": "categorize_period(1601) = ''1500+ AD''"}, {"source": "output/remediation/gold_standard/GOLD_STANDARD.md:79", "url": "output/remediation/gold_standard/GOLD_STANDARD.md", "quote": "| `period_name` | equals `categorize_period(period_start)` |"}, {"source": "WD3 decision (counted, round 1, wd3-r1-b0003)", "decision": "replace", "status": "MISSING", "reasoning": "The park itself, not the prehistoric mounds inside it, is the site named here, and the article states that Charles I created it as a deer park in the 17th century, the same century as his 1625 hunting park and the 1637 enclosure. A century counts as its first year, so the start is 1601 AD. Historic England''s own wording quoted in the same article, a royal deer park with pre C15 origins, leaves any earlier origin undated, and the mounds in the park are only loosely assigned to the Neolithic or Bronze Age.", "value_page": null, "model": "minimax/MiniMax-M3.1-Flash-Preview (MiniMax Code agent)"}]'::jsonb),
